    <article>
      <section id="overview" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
        <h2>What this is</h2>
        <p>ixigo is one of India’s largest travel apps, used by crores of people to book trains. It helps you brilliantly right up to payment. After that, during the hours when a waitlist decides whether you travel at all, it goes quiet. I rebuilt that stretch of the experience as a system rather than a screen. Six states cover the calm path and both failures, with honest odds, a countdown, and a real backup plan.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="problem" class="lt wide">
        <div class="screen">
          <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/00-before.webp" width="512" height="3109" alt="The current ixigo post booking screen, before the redesign" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div><figcaption>Before · the current screen</figcaption></figure>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
            <h2>It starts at 5:40 PM</h2>
            <p>Leela and Vishal are booked on the 12472 Swaraj Express, New Delhi to Nagda, leaving at 21:40. They paid ₹1,401. They have a PNR. What they do not have is a seat.</p>
            <p>They are waitlisted. Somewhere around 5:40 PM today a chart gets prepared and a computer decides whether they travel. Between paying and that moment, the app has almost nothing to say. It shows a line that reads Chart not prepared and a status that has not moved in hours.</p>
            <p>So they do what everyone does. They refresh. They open a third party app to check the odds. They google whether waitlist 23 usually clears.</p>
            <blockquote>They are not looking for information. They are looking for a decision they can make.</blockquote>
            <p>Status, not guidance. The confidence percentage shown at search time disappears here, and the safety net only promises money back.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="research" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What the research showed</p>
        <h2>The detail that settled the direction</h2>
        <p>There is an entire industry of apps built for one job: predicting whether your train ticket will confirm. ConfirmTkt, RailYatri, RailMitra, and more. Millions of downloads between them. They exist because people do not trust the raw status the official app gives them. Someone has already booked their ticket, and they still go somewhere else to find out what it means.</p>
        <blockquote>That is not a feature gap. That is a trust gap.</blockquote>
        <div class="stats">
          <div><b>77%</b><span>of trains on time, and on time officially means within fifteen minutes</span></div>
          <div><b>4 hrs</b><span>before departure, the chart decides whether you travel at all</span></div>
          <div><b>0</b><span>guidance offered in the app during that entire window</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>The safety nets that do exist are quiet or hidden. Vikalp, the alternate train scheme, has to be opted into at booking. The connecting train protection returns money and nothing else. The app is not short on data. It is short on meaning.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="reframe">
        <p class="eyebrow">The reframe</p>
        <h2>Every screen answers one question</h2>
        <p class="big">What do I do now.</p>
        <p>Not what is happening. Not what your status is. What your next move is. A status tracker shows you a state and leaves you to interpret it. A decision support system tells you what the state means and what you can do about it. Same data, completely different job.</p>
        <p>And because a trip is one continuous thing rather than a set of unrelated pages, this is not six screens. It is one hub that changes as the journey moves.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="system" class="lt wide">
        <div style="display:grid;gap:22px;max-width:66ch">
          <p class="eyebrow">The system</p>
          <h2>One hub, six states</h2>
          <p>The same place in the app, answering a different question depending on where the journey is. Two of those states are failures, which is where most redesigns stop and where this one does the most work.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="flowfig"><img class="shot wide" src="img/00-flow.webp" width="1680" height="2040" alt="Flow diagram of the post booking system, from waiting through every outcome after the chart" loading="lazy"><button type="button" class="flowfs" aria-label="Open the flow full screen"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M15 3h6v6M9 21H3v-6M21 3l-7 7M3 21l7-7"/></svg>Full screen</button></figure>
        <dialog class="flowdlg" aria-label="Flow diagram, full screen"><button type="button" class="flowx" aria-label="Close full screen"><svg aria-hidden="true" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M18 6L6 18M6 6l12 12"/></svg></button><div class="flowpan"><img src="img/00-flow.webp" width="1680" height="2040" alt="Flow diagram of the post booking system, from waiting through every outcome after the chart"></div><p class="flowhint">Pinch or double tap to zoom</p></dialog>
        <div class="screens">
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/01-waiting.webp" width="720" height="3183" alt="Waiting state: the odds, a countdown to the chart and a backup plan" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">01 · Waiting · Waitlisted and RAC, before the chart</span><h3>The wait</h3>
              <p>The hardest screen, because nothing is happening yet and that is exactly the problem. Three things went in.</p>
              <p><strong>Honest odds per passenger.</strong> Leela is RAC 5, near certain, green. Vishal is waitlist 24 at 70 percent, blue, moving. Both show movement, because a number that visibly changes tells you the app is watching for you.</p>
              <p><strong>A countdown to the decision.</strong> Seats decided around 5:40 PM today. Naming it turns open ended dread into a finish line.</p>
              <p><strong>A backup held before the outcome.</strong> Not a refund promise. A real plan B, locked in while hope is still alive.</p>
              <p>Nowhere does it say you will get a seat. A waitlist is never certain, and a promise you cannot keep costs more trust than silence does.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><div class="pm one"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/01a-lock.webp" width="720" height="1588" alt="Lock backup sheet: pick a plan B while the waitlist is still uncertain" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">01a</span><h3>The lock, and what it actually costs</h3>
              <p>Asking someone to commit to a backup while they are still hoping is a big ask, so the sheet leads with Nothing is charged now. Here is exactly what you are locking. Three plain lines follow: one Sleeper seat held on the 12138 for both passengers, triggers only if the chart does not confirm you at 5:40 PM, pay only if it triggers. Then the escape hatch, you can change or cancel the backup any time before the chart prepares. Every objection answered before it is raised.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/02-confirmed.webp" width="720" height="2729" alt="Confirmed state: berths assigned for both passengers, with the arrival alarm" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">02 · Confirmed · Chart cleared, berths allotted</span><h3>The good outcome still needs a screen</h3>
              <p>Most redesigns treat success as the state that needs no work. It does. The moment the chart clears, the passenger’s question changes completely, from will I travel to where exactly do I stand on the platform.</p>
              <p>So the screen changes what it emphasises. The odds and the countdown disappear entirely, because they are now answered. The berth becomes the largest thing on each passenger row, S6-34 lower and S6-37 middle, because that is the one piece of information a confirmed passenger is actually looking for. Under it, boarding at New Delhi, Platform 8, departs 9:40 PM, boarding opens 9:10 PM.</p>
              <p>The primary action shifts from locking a backup to tracking the live journey, with setting an arrival alarm underneath. The trip is calm now, so the screen gets calm with it.</p>
              <p>This is what one hub, many states really means. Nothing moved to a different page. The same surface stopped answering a question that no longer exists and started answering the next one.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen flip">
            <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/03-delay.webp" width="720" height="3426" alt="Delay state: running 40 minutes late, with the new platform and coach position" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">03 · Delay and platform · Running late, platform moved</span><h3>The day itself</h3>
              <p>The train is confirmed, running 40 minutes late, and the platform has moved from 8 to 12. Most apps flash a red banner and stop. This screen gives the three things you actually need standing in a station: the new platform, where your coach stops on it, and the revised arrival. Then the choices, with one clearly recommended. Not a menu.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><div class="pm one"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/03a-boarding.webp" width="720" height="1588" alt="Boarding check: the moment the train leaves, asking if you are on board" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">03a · Boarding check · The train has just departed</span><h3>The moment the train leaves</h3>
              <p>Missing your train is the worst failure in Indian rail travel, and no app catches it. So the moment 12472 actually departs, not the scheduled time, the live departure, a gentle check appears. Are you on board?</p>
              <p>Yes opens the live journey. No does not hand you a helpline number. It opens the same rescue engine the failed chart uses.</p>
              <p>This one sheet is the hinge of the whole system. It is the only place the app asks the user something instead of telling them, and it is what makes the difference between a tracker and something that is actually looking out for you.</p>
              <div class="wrong"><span class="k pr">Two things I got wrong first</span><p>I originally triggered it five minutes after the scheduled departure, which is wrong when the average delay runs past half an hour and the train is often still sitting there. And I made it a hard gate, which nags the 95 percent of people who are perfectly fine. It became a soft card on real departure, defaulting to the good path, with a quiet line underneath: still boarding? You can answer in a minute.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/04-onboard.webp" width="720" height="2871" alt="On board tracker: progress station by station and a wake up alarm before arrival" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">04 · On board tracker · Travelling, train on time</span><h3>Eight hours of nothing to do</h3>
              <p>The passenger said yes. Now they are on a train for eight hours overnight, and the only question left is when do I need to be awake.</p>
              <p>The screen becomes a station by station progress line. Departed New Delhi on time at 9:40 PM, currently near Mathura Junction, next stop Agra around 11:10 PM, then Agra Cantt, then arrives Nagda around 5:50 AM tomorrow. Under it, one commitment: we will wake you 30 minutes before your stop.</p>
              <p>This is the state where a flight style boarding tracker would have been the obvious pattern to reach for, and it would have been wrong. Trains do not board on a gate clock and they do not have gate numbers. Real station progress is what a rail passenger actually tracks, so that is what the screen shows.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen">
            <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/05-missed.webp" width="720" height="3364" alt="Missed train rescue: the next trains and buses you can still take, ranked" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">05 · Missed train rescue · The train left without you</span><h3>They said no</h3>
              <p>The screen says it plainly. You missed the Swaraj Express. No softening, no apology copy. Then it immediately does something useful, because the situation is not actually hopeless and most people do not know that.</p>
              <p>The train is near Ghaziabad. It reaches Agra at 11:10 PM, tagged Catchable. Your ticket is valid till Agra. You have about 1h 20m to get there, and if you board there your original ticket still works. That is the recommended option, and it is recommended because it costs nothing.</p>
              <p>Below it, ranked by cost, the 12920 Malwa Express at 11:30 PM with Sleeper seats at ₹430, or a bus at 12:15 AM at ₹620. The primary action is get directions to Agra, with filing for a refund kept available underneath rather than presented as the answer.</p>
              <blockquote>Missing a train is stressful. We will help you get moving.</blockquote>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="screen flip">
            <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/06-chart-failed.webp" width="720" height="3231" alt="Chart failed state: the waitlist did not clear, refund started and backup options shown" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
            <div><span class="k">06 · Chart failed rescue · The waitlist did not clear</span><h3>The chart did not clear</h3>
              <p>The failure state most redesigns skip. The screen says it plainly, no softening. Then, immediately, the refund is already moving. And under that, three ranked ways to still reach Nagda tonight, starting with the seat that was held four hours ago, one tap to take it.</p>
              <blockquote>That is why the backup exists. If you pre committed a plan B while things were still uncertain, the failure stops being a scramble and becomes a handoff.</blockquote>
              <p>One tap and you are booked. Your refund comes separately. The two things are deliberately decoupled, because waiting for money to arrive before you can travel again is the exact anxiety the whole redesign exists to remove.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="addon" class="lt wide">
        <div class="screen flip">
          <figure><div class="pm"><div class="pm-scr" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the screen"><img class="shot phone" src="img/07-home-strip.webp" width="720" height="1584" alt="ixigo home with the live trip strip pinned at the top" loading="lazy"></div><span class="pm-isl" aria-hidden="true"></span></div></figure>
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">Add on</p>
            <h2>Making the hub find the user</h2>
            <p>Everything above assumes the passenger opens My Trips and taps into their booking. An anxious one will. A calm one will not, and the calm one is exactly who needs to know the platform moved.</p>
            <p>So the hub also surfaces itself. A live trip strip sits on the ixigo home screen showing the single most urgent thing about the current state, here Delayed by 40 minutes, next Rewari, under the train name. Tapping it opens whichever state applies, straight into the screen from the sections above.</p>
            <p>It deliberately reuses the Flight Tracker pattern the app already has on that home screen, so it is a placement decision rather than a new component. The urgent state finds the user the moment the app opens instead of waiting two taps deep inside My Trips.</p>
            <p>Labelled clearly as an add on. It is not part of the core post booking flow and the redesign stands without it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

