      <section id="tradeoffs" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What it cost</p>
        <h2>Trade offs</h2>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">01</span><h3>Hierarchy over completeness</h3><p>The main trade off was hierarchy. The decision leads, so the ticket, the payment and the utilities move below it. That is the right call for an anxious waitlisted passenger, who cares about their seat first and the paperwork second. It is arguably the wrong call once the trip is calm and confirmed. I accepted that because the anxious state is where the app is failing hardest right now.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">02</span><h3>Honesty over comfort</h3><p>The second was honesty over comfort. Showing 70 percent means showing that 30 percent exists. That is a harder screen to look at, and it is the only version that survives being wrong.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="assumptions" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">Assumptions and scope</p>
        <h2>What I took as given</h2>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">01</span><p>This is a single direct train, so missed connections do not apply. I swapped that case for the missed train check, which is the real equivalent failure on a direct journey.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">02</span><p>The held backup builds on ixigo’s existing Alternate Travel Plan, reframed from a passive refund into an active seat hold. The honest version is Vikalp made usable, not magic inventory.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">03</span><p>Confidence percentages and live positions are illustrative, and assume access to IRCTC status and running data.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="ai" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">How I used AI</p>
        <h2>A thinking partner, not the designer</h2>
        <p>It moved fast through the searchable and mechanical parts: pulling and sanity checking rail data, mapping the full space of post booking states so I could see the system before committing to screens, generating options to react against, and drafting copy I then cut back.</p>
        <blockquote>What I rejected matters more than what I accepted.</blockquote>
        <div class="fixes">
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Rejected</span></div><div><p>Over reassuring copy, because a broken promise is worse than an honest number.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Rejected</span></div><div><p>A missed connection scenario it proposed. This is a direct train, so inventing a connection would have made the screen lie about the trip.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Rejected</span></div><div><p>More dashboards, because the point was never to show more data.</p></div></div>
          <div class="fix"><div><span class="k pr">Rejected</span></div><div><p>A flight style boarding tracker, because trains do not board on a gate clock and those states meant nothing to a real passenger.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <p>That last one is the useful lesson. The AI gave me a pattern that looked right and was wrong for the domain. Knowing which is which is still the job.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="review" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What the design review changed</p>
        <h2>A screen can be strategically right and operationally useless</h2>
        <p>Practising designers reviewed the first build and pushed back on the information architecture. Their point was that a post booking screen has four things a passenger reaches for, the PNR, the e ticket and QR, the train details, and the ticket details, and the first version had buried two of them.</p>
        <div class="list">
          <div><span class="n">01</span><p>PNR was styled as a subtitle in the header. It is an identifier a passenger reads aloud to a TTE and types into status checks, so it moved onto the journey card as a labelled field with a copy action.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">02</span><p>E ticket and Scan QR sat two scrolls down. Ticket access is time critical at a gate, so both moved into the first fold, directly under the journey card.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">03</span><p>Seat numbers on the confirmed screen carried no emphasis. The berth is now the visual anchor of each passenger row.</p></div>
          <div><span class="n">04</span><p>Platform numbers were showing on the waiting screen before a platform is ever assigned. They now appear only once the information genuinely exists.</p></div>
        </div>
        <blockquote>The designers read it as users. I had been reading it as an argument.</blockquote>
        <p>I had ranked the screen by importance to my argument rather than by when the user needs each thing. Urgency and importance are different axes, and sorting by the wrong one is what buried the essentials.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="next" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">What I would do next</p>
        <h2>Ship the confidence model first</h2>
        <p>It is the smallest change with the largest trust return, and it does not depend on anything else. Then measure the thing that actually indicates success: not engagement, but a drop in refresh frequency.</p>
        <div class="metric"><span class="k">The one metric that matters</span><b>A drop in refresh frequency</b></div>
      </section>
    </article>
